      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the value of \\( b + m \\), we need to use the fact that the lines intersect at the point \\((6, 10)\\). This means that when \\( x = 6 \\), both equations of the lines give the same \\( y \\)-value of 10.\n\nFirst, substitute \\( x = 6 \\) and \\( y = 10 \\) into the equation \\( y = mx + 4 \\):\n\\[\n10 = m \\cdot 6 + 4\n\\]\nSolving for \\( m \\):\n\\[\n10 = 6m + 4\n\\]\n\\[\n10 - 4 = 6m\n\\]\n\\[\n6 = 6m\n\\]\n\\[\nm = 1\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\( x = 6 \\) and \\( y = 10 \\) into the equation \\( y = 3x + b \\):\n\\[\n10 = 3 \\cdot 6 + b\n\\]\nSolving for \\( b \\):\n\\[\n10 = 18 + b\n\\]\n\\[\n10 - 18 = b\n\\]\n\\[\nb = -8\n\\]\n\nNow, we have \\( m = 1 \\) and \\( b = -8 \\). Therefore, the value of \\( b + m \\) is:\n\\[\nb + m = -8 + 1 = -7\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-7}\n\\]",
