      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Given that the lines $y = mx + 4$ and $y = 3x + b$ intersect at the point $(6, 10)$, we can use this point to find the values of $m$ and $b$.\n\nFirst, substitute the coordinates $(6, 10)$ into the equation $y = mx + 4$ to find $m$:\n\n\\[\n10 = 6m + 4.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 4 from both sides gives:\n\n\\[\n6 = 6m.\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by 6 gives:\n\n\\[\nm = 1.\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute the coordinates $(6, 10)$ into the equation $y = 3x + b$ to find $b$:\n\n\\[\n10 = 3(6) + b.\n\\]\n\nCalculating the right side gives:\n\n\\[\n10 = 18 + b.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 18 from both sides gives:\n\n\\[\nb = 10 - 18 = -8.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the value of $b + m$:\n\n\\[\nb + m = -8 + 1 = -7.\n\\]\n\nSo, the value of $b + m$ is \\boxed{-7}. \n\nTo confirm the calculations, we can check the point $(6, 10)$ in both equations. For the first equation $y = mx + 4$ with $m = 1$:\n\n\\[\ny = 1 \\cdot 6 + 4 = 6 + 4 = 10.\n\\]\n\nFor the second equation $y = 3x + b$ with $b = -8$:\n\n\\[\ny = 3 \\cdot 6 - 8 = 18 - 8 = 10.\n\\]\n\nBoth equations are satisfied, confirming that our solution is correct. The value of $b + m$ is indeed \\boxed{-7}.",
